<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mycenaean Kingdom of Laconia</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.) · capital Ayios Vasileios</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mycenaean palace state of the Eurotas valley, the Bronze Age Sparta of Homer's Menelaus, recently revealed by the palace and tablets of Ayios Vasileios.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, Mycenaean elites appear from c. 1500 BCE, as shown by the rich tholos tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vapheio">Vapheio</a> with its gold cups. A palace was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayios-vasileios">Ayios Vasileios</a>, about 12 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>, while the Menelaion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a>, opposite the later city, held a large mansion.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayios-vasileios">Ayios Vasileios</a> since 2008 have found a palace with a large court, stoas, wall paintings and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, recording textiles, personnel and offerings. Laconia also produced the green porphyry stone from Krokeai, exported across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. The kingdom presumably ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> plain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayios-vasileios">Ayios Vasileios</a> palace was destroyed by fire well before the general collapse, and the Menelaion buildings were abandoned c. 1200 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> was largely depopulated afterwards, and Dorian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> later grew nearby. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> remembered Laconia as the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-of-troy">Helen</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:29.85%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 1200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ayios Vasileios</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mycenaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ayios Vasileios palace, Menelaion</dd></dl></aside></div>

